<!-- Exercise 065 - RenderTransformAnimation (intermediate).
     Goal:   Two halves of the same subject. Give one Border a fixed
     RenderTransform and read the matrix it produces; give another an Animation
     that turns it about its own centre, and see what Avalonia puts in
     RenderTransform when a transform sub-property is animated.
     Drills: RenderTransform, ScaleTransform, RenderTransformOrigin, animating
     RotateTransform.Angle from a keyframe Setter, Transform.Value as a Matrix.
     Passes: dotnet test, filtered to Ex065_ (see
     Ex065_RenderTransformAnimation.axaml.cs for the exact command, because an XML
     comment may not hold two hyphens in a row).

     TWO MEASURED FACTS THAT SHAPE THIS EXERCISE.

     First, you cannot animate RenderTransform itself. A keyframe Setter of
     Property="RenderTransform" Value="scale(2.5)" throws at style-attach time
     with "No animator registered for the property RenderTransform". Animate a
     transform SUB-property instead, spelled with its owning type:
     Property="RotateTransform.Angle". Avalonia then builds a TransformGroup for
     the control, holding one transform of every kind, and animates the rotate
     one inside it.

     Second, the rotation's progress is not asserted, and cannot be: nothing
     pumps the animation clock in a headless test and nothing public can be made
     to, since IClock, ClockBase and Animatable.Clock are all internal in Avalonia
     12.1.1. Measured: the angle read 5.594 degrees and was still 5.594 after 300
     ms of real time. So the test asserts the animation is declared correctly and
     that it attached - the TransformGroup only exists because it did - and
     asserts the fixed transform's matrix exactly, which needs no clock at all. -->
<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             x:Class="FeWoLearning.Avalonia.Exercises.Intermediate.Ex065_RenderTransformAnimation">
  <!-- TODO: replace the placeholder below with:

         <StackPanel Spacing="8">
           <StackPanel.Styles>
             <Style Selector="Border#Spinner">
               <Style.Animations>
                 <Animation Duration="0:0:5" IterationCount="Infinite">
                   <KeyFrame Cue="0%">
                     <Setter Property="RotateTransform.Angle" Value="0.0" />
                   </KeyFrame>
                   <KeyFrame Cue="100%">
                     <Setter Property="RotateTransform.Angle" Value="360.0" />
                   </KeyFrame>
                 </Animation>
               </Style.Animations>
             </Style>
           </StackPanel.Styles>

           <Border Name="Spinner"
                   Width="40" Height="40"
                   Background="#FF3366"
                   RenderTransformOrigin="50%,50%" />

           <Border Name="Scaled" Width="40" Height="40" Background="#CCCCCC">
             <Border.RenderTransform>
               <ScaleTransform ScaleX="2" ScaleY="3" />
             </Border.RenderTransform>
           </Border>
         </StackPanel>

       The origin on Spinner is part of the answer, not decoration: the default
       is the top left corner, so without it the Border swings around a corner
       instead of turning in place. Mind the units, which are the trap here.
       Measured: RenderTransformOrigin="0.5,0.5" parses as an ABSOLUTE point half
       a device pixel from the corner, which is not the centre and is very nearly
       the default. Percentages are what make it relative to the control's own
       size, so "50%,50%" is the centre and is what the test asserts, as
       RelativePoint.Center. -->
  <StackPanel>
    <TextBlock Text="TODO" />
  </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
